    "case_summary": "A 12-year-old female patient presented with symptoms consistent with acute appendicitis lasting one day. Physical examination showed tenderness and rebound tenderness localized to the McBurney's point. Routine blood tests and biochemical investigations were normal except for an elevated total leukocyte count of 16,000/ml. The patient underwent an emergency appendectomy. The surgery was uneventful, with the appendix appearing swollen and inflamed. She was discharged the following day. During an outpatient follow-up, a wound infection was identified and managed conservatively. The patient later developed an incisional hernia at the site of the appendectomy, which was subsequently repaired in an elective procedure.\n\nHistopathology analysis of the appendectomy specimen revealed the presence of caseating granulomas and Langhan's giant cells.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
